## 9. Acceptance criteria

The website is ready only when:

- All eight chapters are present and appear in the intended order.
- The story correctly reflects an online connection and the actual sequence of events.
- All 18 candles can be placed and extinguished.
- Cake slices, wishes, dares, and share previews function correctly.
- The gift box opens and transitions into the room.
- The switch illuminates the room and reveals the memory wall.
- Image frames open correctly.
- All three confession responses work without coercive interactions.
- The journal can be edited, copied, and optionally shared.
- The website works on mobile and desktop.
- No broken images, missing assets, dead buttons, fake integrations, or build errors remain.

At completion, provide:

1. A summary of the files created or changed.
2. The commands to run the project locally.
3. The steps to replace placeholders with real images and personal text.
4. Any remaining limitations or features that need external configuration.

Start by inspecting the project, then implement the website end to end. Make reasonable design decisions without repeatedly asking for approval, and prioritise a working, beautiful, complete experience over unnecessary complexity.

# 6. Build it in the right order

Don't try to perfect the 3D room before the rest of the website works. Use this sequence in Antigravity:

1. First: Run the master prompt and get every chapter implemented.
2. Second: Replace the placeholder story text with your actual memories and her nickname.
3. Third: Add the cake, candle, slice-selection, gift and lighting interactions; test each one on mobile.
4. Fourth: Add her photographs and your selected captions, with images she would be comfortable seeing in a personal gift.
5. Fifth: Polish the confession and test all three answers. Check that none of the choices breaks the experience.
6. Finally: Deploy to Vercel or Netlify, test the public URL on your phone, and send it to her on her birthday.

One final creative suggestion: make the birthday celebration the fun part, the room the breathtaking part, and the confession the quiet part. Don't reveal the romantic message too early. Let the story earn that moment.

And because you've only known each other online so far, let the confession express what you genuinely feel without assuming she feels the same. That honesty will make the gift more meaningful than any animation.